.TH CSTRING_C_STR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cstring_c_str \- return a pointer to the null-terminated character data
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cstring.h>

const char *cstring_c_str(cstr s);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a read-only pointer to the internal null-terminated buffer.
The pointer becomes invalid after any mutating operation on the string
that may reallocate the buffer; see NOTES for the exact list.

This function is the recommended bridge when using a cstr with chashmap
or cbstmap. Those containers understand char * keys (with content-based
hashing and comparison) but have no special handling for the cstr type.
Extract the C string first and pass it as the key:

    char *k = (char *)cstring_c_str(my_cstr);
    chmap_insert(map, k, value);

The map copies the string content into its own storage immediately, so
the cstr can be mutated or destroyed afterwards without affecting the
map entry.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s   String to query
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Pointer to null-terminated character data
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Pointer is invalidated by any call that may reallocate (append, prepend,
insert, set, replace, reserve, reset)
Asserts if s is NULL
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_length (3),
.BR cstring_at (3)
